Call an executable's IFUNC through the function its resolver returns, and
let the assembler make one with .type gnu_indirect_function.

An STT_GNU_IFUNC symbol is a resolver: what a call to the symbol must reach
is the function the resolver returns. GNU ld writes an IRELATIVE
relocation for each, which a dynamic loader applies, or in a static
executable glibc's startup through __rela_iplt_start. musl's startup
applies none, and TinyCC 0.9.27 writes none: a static link put the
resolver's address in every reference, so a call ran the resolver and
returned the function's address, and rach2-x86_64-static-link.patch's
direct call to a function an executable defines does the same in a static
or a dynamic link. A dynamic link took the address of a function pointer
or a GOT entry from the resolver too. The assembler ignored the type, so
TinyCC could not make an IFUNC at all.

.type sym, @gnu_indirect_function or STT_GNU_IFUNC now sets the attribute
ifunc, and update_storage gives the ELF symbol that type. In an
executable, build_got_entries takes each reference to a defined IFUNC
before any other rule, the direct call among them, gives the IFUNC a GOT
entry, and points the reference at a stub of .iplt, `jmp *entry`, whose
address is the function's for every reference, a GOT entry's included, as
GNU ld makes it in an executable that is not position independent.

In a dynamic executable the entry has an R_X86_64_IRELATIVE or
R_386_IRELATIVE relocation, made as put_got_entry makes the R_RELATIVE of
a local symbol: it names the IFUNC in symtab_section until
fill_local_got_entries gives it the resolver's address, as the addend on
x86-64 and in the entry on i386. The loader calls the resolver and stores
what it returns, as it does for GNU ld's executable; a resolver runs while
the executable is relocated, before the entries after its own are filled.
An IFUNC that the executable exports, with -rdynamic or to a shared
library that refers to it, was exported as STT_GNU_IFUNC at the resolver,
so dlsym gave the function the resolver returns while the executable's
own references took the stub. ifunc_stub now exports it as STT_FUNC of
size 0 at the stub, as GNU ld does, where it kept the resolver's size, and
the IRELATIVE relocation keeps the resolver's address.
fill_local_got_entries, which gives these relocations their addresses,
read the relocations of a GOT that had none, as an i386 library whose
code takes absolute addresses has, through a null pointer; it now returns.

A static executable has no loader. ifunc_entry_code writes into .iplt code
that calls each resolver and stores what it returns in its entry, keeping
the stack pointer and %edx or %rdx, which the ABI gives the entry point,
and aligning the stack to 16 bytes for each call, and that jumps to the
entry point, whose place in the ELF header it takes. The resolvers thus
run before any constructor, as glibc's static startup runs them; like
there, a resolver must not rely on the C library's initialization. An
executable without an IFUNC links as before.

